# level0 → level1

`main` passes `argv[1]` to `atoi` and compares the result with `0x1a7` (423).
The matching branch changes real and effective IDs to those of the setuid
executable, then executes `/bin/sh`. There is no memory corruption here.

On the RainFall VM:

```sh
ssh -p 4242 level0@<VM_IP> # password: level0
./level0 423
id                         # effective UID is level1
cat /home/user/level1/.pass
```

`objdump -M intel -d ./level0` shows `cmp eax,0x1a7` at `main+25` and the
`setresgid`, `setresuid`, and `execv` calls on the matching branch. The
`flag` file records the level1 password obtained in the VM.
